  "summary": "A state-sponsored architectural style that adapted modernist principles to serve socialist ideology and practical needs.",
  "description": "It combined modernist forms with socialist symbolism and functional requirements, creating buildings that were both technologically advanced and ideologically appropriate. This style represented the socialist bloc's engagement with global modernism while maintaining political messaging.",
  "culturalContext": "Socialist Modernism names the state-sponsored modernism of the Eastern Bloc (1955-1991) — after Khrushchev's 1955 decree against Stalinist excess — in which Soviet, Yugoslav and Romanian architects built hotels, universities, memorials and bus stops in a Soviet-branded international style: expressive concrete canopies, spaceship forms, mosaic panels of workers, prefab panel housing. BACU's documentation project and the 2018 book canonized it; Belgrade's Genex Tower and Kyiv's institutes are icons of a techno-utopianism that age has made elegiac.",
